# id: M62-lock-expiry-off-by-one
# covering-test: TestTheDeadlineIsComparedAgainstTheReadTimestamp
# package: ./kv/
# expect: killed
# power: n/a -- and the reason is a FINDING rather than an exemption. Measured 0 of 300 and then 0 of 100 with the sweep detector, and the census says it is not unreached: applied to the tree, 33 of ~60 census fields move at 40 seeds, TxnLostToResolver going 0 -> 2, which is live coordinators losing transactions to a resolver that had no right to kill them -- the defect itself, counted. So the behaviour changes, the schedule diverges, and NO oracle and no exit criterion says anything. That is section 13.4's surrendered property realised: aborting a transaction is a legal outcome, so every client-facing checker is blind by construction, and the only thing standing between the repository and this defect is TestTheDeadlineIsComparedAgainstTheReadTimestamp. The detector it needs is proposed in DESIGN-A6 section 35 and is not built here
# mutates: a lock is never respected as live -- the expiry test always falls through to roll-back. SYMMETRIC: every replica breaks the same live locks, so the cluster agrees and a transaction is aborted by a competitor that had no right to abort it. Atomicity survives, because a rolled-back transaction is a legitimate outcome; what is lost is that a live coordinator's work is destroyed under it
--- a/kv/resolve.go
+++ b/kv/resolve.go
@@ -87,7 +87,7 @@
 		return ResolveForward, rec.CommitTS, nil
 	case ok:
 		return ResolveBack, hlc.Timestamp{}, nil
-	case readTS.LessEq(l.Deadline):
+	case readTS.Less(l.Deadline) && false:
 		return ResolveWait, hlc.Timestamp{}, nil
 	default:
 		return ResolveBack, hlc.Timestamp{}, nil
